<HowToGuide title="Archetype of One Story" currentGuide="archetype-of-one-story">
  <p>
    Joseph Campbell, author of <em>The Hero with a Thousand Faces</em>, drew a map of something
    remarkable: all the world&rsquo;s great stories follow one storyline. Every myth, fairy tale,
    and legend &mdash; regardless of language or culture &mdash; shares the same backbone. He
    called it the Hero&rsquo;s Journey; at DT:FC we use the more inclusive name
    <strong>Wayfarer&rsquo;s Journey</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Wayfarer starts at Home. A Call disturbs the ordinary world. A Gate opens and the Road
    of Trials begins. Something transformative happens at the middle &mdash; the Nigredo, the
    dissolving that must occur before a new self can emerge. A second Road of Trials follows.
    The Gate closes. The Wayfarer Returns Home, changed.
  </p>
  <p>
    A theatre experience transports the audience to that timeless place. The protagonist moves
    through the cycle during the play, though not necessarily in order &mdash; a play might begin
    at the Nigredo and use flashbacks to reveal earlier stations, as DT:FC&rsquo;s children&rsquo;s
    play <em>OCEAN</em> does.
  </p>

  <WayfarersJourneyWheel />

  <h2>The eight stations</h2>

  <h3>1. Home</h3>
  <p>
    Home is the current situation &mdash; the ordinary world before the story truly begins.
    Archetypes we know live here: the Giant, the Queen and King, the Fool, the Wise Woman. No one
    invented these figures; they appear to be stored inside each person like a common library whose
    books remain unopened until a story activates them. Establish Home in as few strokes as
    possible so the departure carries weight.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Call</h3>
  <p>
    The Call is the disturbance &mdash; a summons to seek a cure, an answer, something the
    Wayfarer can no longer ignore. It is rarely welcome. Most heroes refuse it at least once, and
    that refusal is part of the story. The Call creates the hunger that drives everything that
    follows.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Gate In</h3>
  <p>
    Crossing the Gate In begins the Wayfarer&rsquo;s journey in earnest. This is the threshold
    between the ordinary world and the world of the story, and it is usually guarded: a giant, a
    river, a wise woman who asks three questions. Once past the Gate, the ordinary world&rsquo;s
    rules no longer apply.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Road of Trials</h3>
  <p>
    The first arc of tests, allies, and enemies. This part of the Road usually defines the
    problem, identifies the enemies, and tests the Wayfarer&rsquo;s resolve. The hero learns the
    shape of the new world and discovers what they&rsquo;re actually made of.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Nigredo</h3>
  <p>
    Nigredo is the alchemical name for the darkest part of the journey &mdash; the cocoon, wherein
    the old person is dissolved and a new person emerges. The DNA holds true, but there is a
    notable difference between the caterpillar and the butterfly. In children&rsquo;s myths this is
    often literal darkness: the belly of the wolf, the bottom of the well, the sleep from which no
    one wakes. Campbell believed transformation is not on a time clock; the Nigredo takes as long
    as it takes.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Road of Trials II</h3>
  <p>
    After the Nigredo, the Wayfarer is different. The second Road of Trials provides opportunities
    to practice being the butterfly &mdash; the transformed person. The tests continue, but the
    terms have changed; the Wayfarer is now capable of things they weren&rsquo;t before.
  </p>

  <h3>7. Gate Out</h3>
  <p>
    The practice period comes to an end. There is a graduation, a crossing back over the threshold
    &mdash; usually with something won: the Water of Life, the stolen fire, the answer to the
    question. The Gate Out is the moment the Wayfarer commits to carrying the gift home.
  </p>

  <h3>8. Return</h3>
  <p>
    Home again &mdash; preparing and going to the place where the exterior life (family, work,
    friends) is integrated by the transformed person. It may be an entirely new place with
    different people. The story ends, but the Wayfarer&rsquo;s transformation persists, and so the
    cycle begins again: settling into and living the new life until the next Call.
  </p>

  <h2>Using the wheel in your script</h2>
  <p>
    It&rsquo;s possible to build a play with a scene for each station of the arc. Mark where your
    source tale hits each one. Some tales visit all eight explicitly; many compress two or three
    into a single scene. That&rsquo;s fine &mdash; the wheel is a shape, not a scaffold to follow
    slavishly. It&rsquo;s also useful sometimes to begin elsewhere, say at the Road of Trials, and
    summarize the earlier stations.
  </p>
  <p>
    Missing stations can cause holes in the narrative; the wheel helps you spot them early.
    Skillfully, those gaps can also be filled by the audience&rsquo;s own archetypal understanding
    &mdash; they&rsquo;ve heard this story before, in a thousand forms.
  </p>
  <p>
    See the
    <a href="/childrens-theatre/how-to/create-a-script/">Create a Script</a>
    guide for how to build a scene out of each station once you&rsquo;ve mapped them.
  </p>
</HowToGuide>
